    {
        "content": "<p>If I remember correctly, the meaning of <code>omit</code> is \"undo <code>include</code>\", rather than \"omit this variable\".</p>",
        "id": 613680597,
        "sender_full_name": "Damiano Testa",
        "timestamp": 1785426758
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This combines with the way in which <code>def</code>s see all the available <code>variable</code>s, instead of just the ones needed for the statement to type check and then <code>cases</code> \"touches\" the <code>omit</code>ted variable, leading to the confusion above.</p>",
        "id": 613680900,
        "sender_full_name": "Damiano Testa",
        "timestamp": 1785426837
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>See also <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/.60omit.60.20doesn.27t.20work\">#lean4 &gt; &#96;omit&#96; doesn't work</a></p>",
        "id": 613739252,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1785445241
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It would be nice if <code>omit</code> solved this, but even it did, this would just be a workaround. Surely the behaviour I’ve identified is a bug?</p>",
        "id": 613740376,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Nelson",
        "timestamp": 1785445588
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>How is <code>cases</code> touching the red herring variable?</p>",
        "id": 613740457,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Nelson",
        "timestamp": 1785445623
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In a <code>def</code>, all of the global variables are available in the tactic state, and unfortunately <code>cases</code>/<code>induction</code> work in such a way that they touch all available variables.</p>\n<p>It is a bit better in the body of a <code>theorem</code>, where this wouldn't happen.</p>",
        "id": 613742124,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1785446246
    }
]